Fictional EP Tracklist

Track 1: The Cast-Iron Skillet Sings Ice
- Story & Context: During a force-ten gale at Cape Mercer that severed the main diesel line, Keeper Jonas Eide kept his station awake by boiling frozen cod fat over a blowtorch while blowing vicious, overdriven alto flute arpeggios into the distress microphone. The sound of spitting animal grease against blistering cast iron bled directly into the high-gain vocal mic, inadvertently inventing the genre’s signature biting rhythm section while terrified radio men on three stranded icebreakers listened in bewilderment. He locked into a furious one-chord bop pattern for seven continuous hours until the storm cleared, shouting into the foghorn horn assembly: "Keep the copper pan hot until the cutter breaks through."
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Macro close-up of a heavy cast-iron pan crackling with white rendered fat on a red-hot iron stove, surrounded by sea-salt crust, an antique wooden flute resting on the zinc kitchen counter, arctic storm frost blooming across the adjacent iron porthole, moody cinematic chiaroscuro lighting.

Track 2: "Did You Salt The Kerosene?"
- Story & Context: Confined to the six-by-nine galley of Hopen Island Light for four sunless months, flutist Astrid Lind cracked under the continuous ticking of the station's rotational timer and the constant bickering of her relief engineer over burnt chicory coffee. In a quiet state of delirium, she began playing an obsessively repeated twelve-bar blues motif backwards on a detuned wooden flute, tapping her wedding ring against an empty enamel kettle to establish a relentless, minimalist swing. When the supply ship finally arrived in spring, they found the logbook filled only with flute fingerings and her final broadcast transcript: "I put three pinches of drift-ash where the sugar used to sleep."
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: An enamel coffee pot and a half-eaten loaf of dark rye bread on a scratched zinc table inside a claustrophobic wooden nautical cabin, an open logbook covered in frantic musical notation, cold blue northern twilight pouring through the window, gritty analog film aesthetic.

Track 3: Ground-Wave Braise
- Story & Context: Legend spread across the Murmansk shipping lanes of a ghost broadcast emanating from an automated navigational spit near Bear Island, where an abandoned beacon was rumored to transmit complex modal hard-bop loops whenever heavy blizzards swept the channel. Skeptical radio inspector Torstein Vang sailed out to dismantle the transmitter, only to find the radio room empty except for the automated lens spinning in sync with a looping shortwave tape player wired to an old brass flute suspended over a venting radiator. As the frequency resonated across forty nautical miles of frozen sea, Vang simply sat down at the galley table, warmed his hands on the dead stove, and whispered into the transmitter: "Feed the iron stove bone-fat and the reef will stay asleep."
